  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =({x}^{2}-3){e}^{x}+2x\\,{e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ for all real $x,$ this reduces to\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-3>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x+3)(x-1)$ has roots at $x=-3$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039985/assets/9b847390fb6de568144d309c45fb8c904475bdde79a46a90ec0fd4426a71b57b.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x+3)(x-1)$ is positive when $x<-3$ or $x>1.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x>1.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
